WHY THIS PROBE EXISTS -- incant/kant8T's K2 CANNOT ANSWER THE QUESTION IT ASKS.

K2 is the standing gate's row for "does an argument dodge the restore?" and it
reads 7, which has been taken as YES for direct recursion -- the asymmetry
K6c's own comment states in so many words: "In DIRECT recursion an argument
DODGES the emptying ... In MUTUAL recursion there is no bracket at all."

K2's body is:

    k2 argument code={
        if k2depth > 0;
            k2depth = k2depth - 1;
            k2(argument);
        return argument;
        };

IT PASSES THE SAME ARGUMENT DOWN. So the inner activation binds the value the
outer already had, and restoring 7 over 7 is indistinguishable from never
trampling at all. A trampled run and a clean run produce THE SAME 7.

That is this project's own anti-vacuity rule, unapplied: a constant the default
could also produce asserts nothing, and every zero-expecting row wants a
non-zero sibling. K6c is the honest form of the same question -- it hands the
inner activation a DIFFERENT node and reads argument.taG rather than the node
-- and K6c comes back TRAMPLED.

So the direct-vs-mutual asymmetry may be real or may be an artifact of K2's
shape, and NOTHING IN THE FLEET DISCRIMINATES. This probe is K2 with the one
defect removed: a different inner argument, and .taG as the witness (printing a
group node prints its ATTRIBUTE COUNT -- a legal-looking number in the same
range as the answer, which is the instrument failure K6d was built to record).

WHAT THE ANSWER DECIDES, and this is why it runs before the frame bind and not
after:

  ROW 1 = k2xBig    the asymmetry is REAL. Direct and mutual recursion differ,
                    so the argument channel has TWO mechanisms and a fix aimed
                    at one leaves the other standing.

  ROW 1 = k2xSmall  THE ASYMMETRY IS AN ARTIFACT. Direct recursion tramples too
                    and always has; K2 could not see it. One mechanism explains
                    both rows, the fix is one place, and K2 is owed a re-pin
                    plus an honest sibling.

Either way the gate gains a row it does not have today.
